            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to smoke, then the Latin for to go up in flames."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: sīphō, sīphōnis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sīphō, sīphōnis — \"a fire pump; a siphon\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Oppidum nūllum sīphōnem habēbat. The town had no fire pump."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Masculine, 3rd declension, a Greek word. A Roman governor once wrote that a burning town had no sīphō and no buckets."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sīphō was borrowed from Greek siphōn, \"a pipe\" (Lewis & Short): a small pipe, a drinking tube, and a fire engine. English siphon comes from the same Greek word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
